<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lebus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebus</a> is a small town on the Oder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> (Oder). Once a Slavic stronghold, it became in the 12th century the seat of a Polish bishopric under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, which later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furstenwalde">Fürstenwalde</a>, and it gave its name to the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-lebus">Land of Lebus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lebus.jpg" alt="Lebus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Totzauer · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lebus_-_Unterstadt_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebus</a> lies on a ridge on the west bank of the middle Oder, about 10 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> (Oder), in the Märkisch-Oderland District. Since 1945 the river has marked the eastern German border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The town was the centre of the historical region known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-lebus">Lubusz Land</a>, which gives its name to the present-day Polish Lubusz Voivodeship. Settlement in the area has been traced back about 3,000 years, and the ridges gave natural defence and led to fortifications being built on them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From about the 7th century the area was inhabited by Slavic Leubuzzi tribes, and their lands on both sides of the Oder became known as Terra Lebusana. The region was under Polish control by 966, under Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a>. Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a> mentioned the settlement as a town in a chronicle written between 1012 and 1018, and 13th-century chronicles called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebus</a> the key to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>. The Diocese of Lebus was founded in 1124–25 under Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a>, dedicated to Saint Adalbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, to convert the resistant Liutizi tribes.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-lebus">Lubusz Land</a> became part of the Duchy of Silesia in 1138, and in 1163 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> granted it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> to the Silesian Piasts. German colonisation proceeded through the 13th century, and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry the Bearded</a> granted the citizens town privileges in 1226. In 1248–49 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-the-bald">Bolesław II Rogatka</a> pledged town and land to the Ascanian margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and Archbishop Wilbrand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. Brandenburg took control, and the town became a stop on trade routes from the Baltic coast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1373 troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> destroyed the cathedral, and the seat of the bishopric moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furstenwalde">Fürstenwalde</a>.</p><p>The people became Lutheran in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and the bishopric was secularised in 1555 after the death of the last Catholic bishop, Georg von Blumenthal. Lebus has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> since 1701. In the last stage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the town, including its medieval centre, was almost completely destroyed in the Battle of the Seelow Heights. The parish church was restored in 1954.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebus</a> is the administrative seat of the Amt Lebus. Its coat of arms shows a wolf carrying a sheep in its mouth, and the Turmberg offers a view of the Oder valley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lebus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
